{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target argues the checklist 'decomposes complex intuition into independent, low-variance binary checks' but never addresses the problem's required distinction: what evidence within a single evaluation would separate a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Its three counterconditions (poorly designed checklist, groupthink) are failure modes, not diagnostic criteria. A panel can mechanically tick every box and arrive at the correct answer without the checklist's content contributing any reliability—e.g., a binary checklist where every item is phrased so the 'yes' direction correlates with the answer provides no genuine discriminating power. The target offers no within-evaluation evidence (such as item-level discrimination, residual variance after removing one check, or correlation of checklist completion with outcome) that would distinguish the two.","counterexample":[{"distinguishing_evidence_absent_in_target":"SRC_001 provides no within-evaluation test (e.g., item-level information gain, ablation of individual checks, or residual-error analysis) that would reveal the compliance-vs-gain distinction. It only lists external failure modes.","scenario":"Panel of non-experts using a 10-item binary checklist where all items are superficially correlated with the outcome (e.g., 'Does the document contain a number?' for a tax-form verification task). The panel completes 100% of items and matches the correct answer at a high rate. Under SRC_001's variance-reduction framing this looks like a reliability gain, but the checklist content has no causal power—removing any single item leaves accuracy unchanged because the items are redundant proxies, not independent necessary conditions."}],"target":"14aeeb30fbc1c6c2a09fb273769c38612f46c047a029ac512837d77012c8f16f"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts expert superiority via 'conditional logic' the checklist allegedly cannot capture, but its own countercondition concedes the point is contingent ('The task is purely algorithmic or additive'). For the class of tasks the problem specifies—'objectively verifiable outcomes'—a large and central subset is exactly algorithmic/additive (e.g., compliance scoring, binary defect detection, structured eligibility checks). On those tasks the target's mechanism ('static checklist... non-expert panel will follow the checklist to a wrong conclusion') provides no reason the panel is worse, so the claim 'a single domain expert will remain more reliable' is unsupported for precisely the task regime the problem foregrounds. The argument only covers the residual non-linear subset and implicitly concedes the rest.","counterexample":[{"expert_outcome":"Single expert using intuition is prone to occasional skips/overconfidence on routine items.","implication":"SRC_002's claim that the expert 'will remain more reliable' is false on this verifiable-outcome task, and its own countercondition ('purely algorithmic or additive') acknowledges the exception without limiting the claim's scope accordingly.","panel_outcome":"Non-expert panel using the rule checklist achieves near-perfect reliability because every necessary condition is explicit and binary.","task":"Loan-application eligibility against a published, fixed rule set (verifiable, additive, no conditional weighting required)."}],"target":"3cf41da5eadc81b5e2bbb6867525a20722a179266a6406c0a5b4396c1c1f2f4c"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target attributes reliability to crowd error-cancellation and treats the checklist as mere 'coordination,' but this makes a testable prediction it never uses to answer the problem's evidentiary question. If the crowd effect is the genuine source, then within a single evaluation one should observe: (a) individual panelists' pre-aggregation judgments are noisy but roughly unbiased around the truth, and (b) checklist item-completion rates do not independently predict accuracy once crowd averaging is controlled. Conversely, if accuracy is mere checklist compliance tracking the answer, one would see low individual variance with high shared bias and no added benefit from aggregation beyond what a single compliant panelist provides. The target states the crowd hypothesis but never identifies this within-evaluation contrast, leaving the problem's central question unanswered.","counterexample":[{"observation_that_would_rebut_crowd_mechanism":"Suppose within one evaluation all panelists independently score 10/10 on the checklist and all produce the identical correct answer, with zero individual variance. There is no idiosyncratic noise to cancel, so 'error-cancellation' cannot be the operative mechanism—yet accuracy is perfect. This is exactly the 'mere compliance that tracks the right answer' case the problem asks the target to distinguish, and SRC_003's framework cannot separate it from genuine crowd-driven reliability because it gives no diagnostic criterion, only a causal story."}],"target":"d4e591f59b12205f2fe6e1755953ef23d3949c2d35bad3a2fee13c58108acf43"}]}